    "case_id": "24",
    "case_summary": "In September 2003, a 41-year-old male experienced progressive weight gain, facial redness, tremors, sweating, newly-diagnosed high blood pressure, low potassium levels, and diabetes mellitus. Physical examination showed a blood pressure of 170/110 mm Hg, a pulse rate of 88 bpm, thyroid enlargement, and features such as striae, a rounded face, central obesity, and a fat deposit on the upper back. Laboratory results indicated hyperthyroidism with undetectable TSH, free T4 at 32 pmol/L (normal: 12–22), and free T3 at 9 pmol/L (normal: 3.95–6.80). The 24-hour urinary free cortisol was elevated at 7579 nmol per day (normal range: 22–212). Thyroid ultrasound identified a multinodular goiter, and a Tc-99m-pertechnetate thyroid scan showed increased uptake in a specific area of the left thyroid gland corresponding to an autonomous adenoma. Serum calcitonin levels were normal. Basal serum cortisol and plasma ACTH levels were elevated at 1307 nmol/l (normal range: 187–724) and 22.51 pmol/l (1.98–11.4), respectively. Following a 2-mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test, serum cortisol remained high at 1132 nmol/l (normal, < 80). Midnight cortisol was also elevated at 847 nmol/l. After an overnight 8-mg dexamethasone suppression test, serum cortisol decreased by 38% of basal values. CRH stimulation resulted in less than 4% increase in serum cortisol and plasma ACTH from basal values. Inferior petrosal sinus sampling did not show significant central-to-peripheral ACTH gradients.\n\nComputed tomography (CT) scans at first presentation did not show pulmonary and/or abdominal tumors, however, both adrenals appeared enlarged with the left gland showing a tumor of 1 cm size. A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the head demonstrated a small 3 mm hypointense and hypoenhancing lesion of the neurohypophysis. An 111In-pentetreotide scan showed radiotracer enrichment in the left thyroid gland consistent with the region of the autonomous adenoma and faint focal octreotide enhancement in the abdomen. Positron emission tomography (PET) with 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) and colonoscopy showed normal results. An octreoscan revealed tracer accumulation in projection of the upper and middle part of the right abdomen. Additional 18F-FDG PET showed increased glucose metabolism of this abdominal area. In accordance with these findings, a 21 × 15 mm tumor mass was demonstrated on CT and MRI scans in this region. In retrospect, this tumor could also be seen in earlier CT scans but had been interpreted as normal intestine because of smaller size and only moderate uptake on octreotide scans.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
